# Sub-tick interpolation of the animated terrain
# (scripts/check_feeder_subtick.sh --path terrain, --path terrain-tanim).
#   <source> | <V> | [rect=<x0>,<y0>,<x1>,<y1> ...] [step=<px>] [itol=<px>]
#
# This path's knobs, OPENPETE_TERRAIN_VANIM and OPENPETE_TERRAIN_TANIM, do not
# suppress the terrain feed: they snap one channel of the sub-tick present to
# the newest tick's state. The canonical presents are therefore identical
# between the two legs and only the intermediate present differs, so the legs
# are intermediate presents alone and their difference is exactly the pixels
# that channel's interpolation moved. Both legs select the terrain path, so no
# other producer can enter it.
#
# Read per cell: the differing-pixel count at each alpha. alpha 1 must differ
# in no pixel (every interpolation term is zero at the tick's own instant),
# alpha 0 must differ in more than `step` pixels (the channel moved something
# this cell can see), and alpha 0.5 separates a channel that carries
# the interval from one that holds its tick state: the latter renders the same
# present in both legs and the count collapses to zero.
#
# The whole frame is the cell where no rect is named: the render-path
# selection has already isolated the terrain.
#
# moving-terrain: the Artisans water surface, the level the movie is named
# for, at the two presents the presentation corpus also pins. A third row on
# toasty-moving-terrain-z-fighting at V=3110 was dropped: no animated surface
# stands in that frame and both legs render the same present at every alpha,
# which the floor reports as VACUOUS rather than as a snap.
moving-terrain.bk2 | 2262 |
